// Practice quiz: Enums
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/classes-and-objects/enums

::o1 use a constant::enum Day \{ MON, TUE, WED \} How do you refer to the Tuesday constant? {
~new Day("TUE")#Not quite. Enum objects cannot be created with new; the constants are the only ones.
~"TUE"#Not quite. That is a String, not a Day.
~Day[1]#Not quite. An enum type is not an array; Day.values()[1] would be needed for that.
=Day.TUE#Correct. Each constant is a fixed object you reach through the enum name.
}

::o1 valueOf case::enum Day \{ MON, TUE, WED \} What does Day.valueOf("Mon") do? {
~It returns Day.MON#Not quite. "Mon" does not match MON exactly.
~It returns null#Not quite. A missing name is reported with an exception, not null.
=It throws IllegalArgumentException#Correct. valueOf needs the exact constant name, and names are case-sensitive.
~It does not compile#Not quite. The call compiles; the problem appears when it runs.
}

::o2 constructor runs::enum Coin \{ PENNY(1), DIME(10); private final int cents; Coin(int cents) \{ this.cents \= cents; \} \} When does the Coin constructor run? {
=Once for each constant, when the enum is first set up#Correct. Each constant is created a single time; your code never calls the constructor.
~Every time Coin.DIME is used#Not quite. Using a constant reuses the one existing object.
~When you write new Coin(10)#Not quite. new cannot be used with an enum; it does not compile.
~Never, because enums cannot have constructors#Not quite. Enums can declare constructors to set their fields.
}

::o2 public constructor::Can an enum declare a public constructor? {
~Yes, like any class#Not quite. Enum constructors are restricted on purpose.
~It must be public#Not quite. It is private even when you write no modifier.
~Only if the enum has no fields#Not quite. The rule applies to every enum constructor.
=No; an enum constructor must not be public or protected#Correct. The language forbids it, because only the constants may create enum objects.
}

::o3 int constants::setLevel(int level) uses static final int LOW \= 0, HIGH \= 1. What does changing it to setLevel(Level level), with enum Level \{ LOW, HIGH \}, add? {
~It makes the program run faster#Not quite. The benefit is type safety, not speed.
~It lets callers pass any int#Not quite. That is the problem the enum removes.
=The compiler rejects any argument that is not a Level, such as setLevel(7)#Correct. Only the declared constants can be passed.
~Nothing; the two versions behave the same#Not quite. With int, setLevel(7) compiles; with Level it does not.
}

::o3 String typo::A method takes String status, and a caller passes "CLOSD" instead of "CLOSED". When is the mistake found? {
~At compile time#Not quite. The compiler cannot know which Strings are valid statuses.
=Not by the compiler; only when the program misbehaves while running#Correct. Any String compiles; with an enum, Status.CLOSD would be a compile error.
~Never; Java corrects the spelling#Not quite. Java does no such correction.
~At compile time, because the String is in capitals#Not quite. Capital letters mean nothing special in a String.
}
